      "id": 1,
      "text": "Petersburg takes its name from freedman Peter Laws, who built a log cabin there after the Civil War.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://history.ky.gov/markers/petersburg"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Freed slave Eliza Curtis Hundley Tevis farmed in Petersburg around 1820 and later bought land in Wet Woods before the Civil War.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://history.ky.gov/markers/petersburg"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In the 19th century, Petersburg became a coach stop between Poplar Level, Shepherdsville, and Louisville, complete with a post office, hotel, shops, and homes.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21,
